    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                "word": "proleptic",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "b0ac8cc3-6ac0-4052-a41a-7a627ca01ecc",
                        "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more proleptic"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "a83b402e-0ba1-444f-bba7-90cba3e74d87",
                        "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most proleptic"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "01c8e985-d1e1-3bb8-bec5-e1e7360fa644",
                        "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Extrapolated to dates prior to its first adoption; of those used to adjust to or from the Julian calendar or Gregorian calendar."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "of a calendar"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4fc381b-5e17-408c-827f-e14c3d0ca72b",
                                "sense_id": "01c8e985-d1e1-3bb8-bec5-e1e7360fa644",
                                "text": "The Julian proleptic calendar is formed by applying the rules of the Julian calendar to times before Caesar's reform, and the Julian date (JD) specifies the particular instant of a day by ending the Julian day number with the fraction of the day elapsed since the preceding Greenwich noon."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "01081af8-ada8-484c-9756-482a478b7fc7",
                                "sense_id": "01c8e985-d1e1-3bb8-bec5-e1e7360fa644",
                                "text": "The proleptic Gregorian calendar is produced by extending the Gregorian calendar backwards to dates preceding its official introduction in 1582."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1a39f395-df85-4289-a1a0-0015134594bd",
                                "sense_id": "01c8e985-d1e1-3bb8-bec5-e1e7360fa644",
                                "text": "The .NET epoch is midnight at the start of January 1st, AD 1, although that's AD 1 in a proleptic Gregorian calendar, which refers to even more complexity we haven't talked about yet."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                        "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Anticipatory; prescient or forward-looking."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ae515022-5f4e-4f18-b0bf-0af7ded80036",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "A far-seeing or proleptic wisdom."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2364e431-7d50-462f-a658-2dc179a9a5eb",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "In contrast with physical death, spiritual death might be called metaphorical. It becomes proleptic when contrasted with the second and final death."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ebf7e86b-1af0-4eda-8a77-4d40188a1927",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "Herbert Gorman’s life of Joyce was written not only when Finnegans Wake was a long way from completion but with the handicap of the subject himself insisting on a hagiography featuring a prolonged, if proleptic, martyrdom."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a2796769-11e1-48a7-8862-c6f7bc2cfae4",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "In World Two, Jesus can be seen as the proleptic event, giving promise of God's vindication of creation in and through history."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3e1744d1-ed39-4eac-92fc-6ebe9d4f194e",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "In a world of youth idolatry, we easily lose our proleptic quality after the first peak, and become regressive, looking back toward the peaks already passed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "30643c54-09cb-44b9-b260-3247f9c44da8",
                                "sense_id": "fe4b554d-2fde-39b0-a306-e62b2eeef0a4",
                                "text": "Viewing the commons as a vehicle for a new world order, Randal Joy Thompson proposes ‘proleptic leadership’, which envisions how leaders will continue to be essential as the custodians of responsible agency and conscious choice."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                        "entry_id": "d0cf9ba3-e7f4-3d29-9340-3373bf9c8101",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Exhibiting or pertaining to prolepsis (any sense)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ea378048-a1d8-4b0b-bd7e-5d3a3ede34dd",
                                "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                                "text": "a 'proleptic' epithet describing the result of the hunt, means literally 'with leafage broken' and is formed from the stem of ἀγνύναι."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8529d644-12c4-432a-aaa2-849c12ba88a9",
                                "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                                "text": "There was no proleptic suffix in Moabite, according to the present evidence."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7b9f2695-25b6-4327-97d5-a8cdae86aa54",
                                "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                                "text": "Starting in the spring of the second year, one terminal indeterminate floral shoot ( I ) was included at every terminal proleptic shoot ( L ) that arose from each proleptic and sylleptic shoot in the summer and full flush of the previous year."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fc661310-48a7-459c-a5b8-868d680cae3e",
                                "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                                "text": "Submission, as is fitting for a dystopia written in the mode of the “not yet”, ends in a proleptic future tense, speaking of what will come for François and (with rather less authorial interest) for the people of France."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fe78d4ec-5a44-439a-8f77-9fc057bb264f",
                                "sense_id": "e53a22a8-a1ce-388d-a012-8dbfb646ecdd",
                                "text": "8) Be proleptic, a word that comes from the Greek for “anticipation.” That is, get the better of the major objection to your argument by raising and answering it in advance."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
